machine sm_120

# A contraction: the first kernel in this language whose traffic per output is an expression
# in a launch extent rather than a constant.
#
# `contract sum p : k` declares an axis that is walked and combined, not one the space
# iterates. It is not a `reduce`: a reduction combines across threads through shared memory and
# its tree order is part of the contract, while a contraction combines inside one thread, in a
# register, in loop order. There is no tree and nothing to choose.
#
# `c[i, j] = ...` assigns once per output. The accumulation is what `contract` declares; the
# body states the term being accumulated. An `+=` would put the schedule back in the body,
# which is the thing this language exists not to do.
#
# THIS DOES NOT COMPILE YET, on purpose. Each block loads 2T^2 elements per step over K/T
# steps, so 2K/T + 1 elements move per output, and `Cost` carries constants. The front end
# accepts the syntax and refuses to cost it rather than publishing the constant part of an
# expression -- which would be wrong by a factor of K. ADR-0018 step 2 replaces the refusal
# with the symbolic form, and step 5 measures it.
#
# The asymptote is `T/4` flop/byte. At `tile 32, 32` that is 8, against a ridge of 42.9 on this
# machine, and it cannot be raised: T^2 threads per block against MAX_THREADS_PER_BLOCK = 1024
# caps T at 32. The thread count binds before the shared memory does, which is worth knowing
# because the obvious guess is the memory.
kernel matmul(m: u32, n: u32, k: u32,
              a: [f32; m, k], b: [f32; k, n], c: [f32; m, n])
    space i, j : m, n
    contract sum p : k
    tile 32, 32
    intensity asymptotic 8.0

    stream a : dram -> smem -> reg
    stream b : dram -> smem -> reg
    stream c : dram -> reg, drain

    at reg:
        c[i, j] = a[i, p] * b[p, j]
